target:    tests/docker/build-packet.py
gate:      composer test:seal-negative
kind:      construct-removal
expect:    T12 expected exit 5 and 'seal-literal'
rationale: Stops handing the audit this run's sealed literals. Every structural rule still applies and the packet still builds, so the only observable difference is an _arm_fingerprint VALUE surviving into the packet - the string that identified the OLD arm of R20260824-a51bf2 on sight. Deliberately not covered by a generic hex rule: the same capture carries Unix timestamps that any such rule reads as refs, which is why the literals exist at all.
---
--- a/tests/docker/build-packet.py
+++ b/tests/docker/build-packet.py
@@ -72,7 +72,7 @@
                                       sort_keys=True,separators=(",",":"))+"\n")
 sealed_literals.chmod(0o600)
 audit=Path(__file__).with_name("scrub-audit.sh")
-result=subprocess.run([str(audit),str(run/"packet"),"--literals",str(sealed_literals)],check=False)
+result=subprocess.run([str(audit),str(run/"packet")],check=False)
 if result.returncode:
     shutil.rmtree(run/"packet"); raise SystemExit(result.returncode)
 
